  "worked_solution": "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{\u2032}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ by ${90}^{\u2218}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{\u2218}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ by ${90}^{\u2218}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{\u2032}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,j}{||{r}^{\u2032}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.$",
